ic antibody as a result of this  they get signal amplification  which gives them the capability for  further improvements  the dnb ctx the tx for high throughput  can do 30x whole genome sequencing for  just a hundred dollars  while the current generation the dna  nanoballs is 200 nanometers in size  and that gets a sufficient signal but  because the antibodies have multiple  floors  on it it should be possible and rodney  dramanchik was talking about this  to make even smaller nanoballs that are  just a hundred nanometers in size  when they're that size you'll get an  even greater sequence output  so there's the capability of getting  genomes for significantly less than even  one hundred dollars competition is good  as bgi rams towards providing a genome  for a hundred dollars all  in illumina is going to have no choice  but to try to compete  or to try to inhibit vgi from selling  machines due to potential patent issues  which  are questionable we have seen a  reduction in whole genome sequencing  from 3 billion dollars  to 600 or less in just 19 years with the  capability  of the hundred dollar genome being very  close well once we have the hundred  dollar genome how long until it's even  improved further we've gone  from million-fold improvement in  sequence output  from just 20 years ago and we're almost  at the dawn of the hundred dollar genome  and with some of the  advances that roddy draunchick is  talking about we could see a sub hundred  dollar genome  why is the hundred dollar genome the  turning point more than anything else  for many years the target seemed to be  the thousand dollar genome  well the first reason that it's a true  turning point is when genomes just cost  a hundred dollars it really facilitates  population based genome sequencing  studies much more rapidly  the plans to sequence 5 million  individuals  in the genome england project will  cost considerable amount of money when  it costs 1 000 to 200 000  per individual but if it costs just a  hundred dollars  the possibility is that it's going to be  easier to do  less expensive and many other countries  will do similar efforts  and studies like the one from genomics  england and the national health service  will reveal the value of whole genome  information to direct health care  and at 100 or less whole genome  sequencing could become a routine part  of our medical record  and this will really get us much more  towards a fully envisioned personalized  medicine  more importantly we can start to  transform the paradigm of health care of  one where people are treated when they  have disease  instead to having a better understanding  of who might develop disease  based on genome sequence and we may also  have the capability of  basically having healthier populations  based upon  information from genome sequence and  possibly other information like what  would be ideal diets  would be ideal exercise regimens which  might be very much dependent on the  genome that you've actually inherited  well what aren't we seeing with all of  these first generation massively  parallel sequencing technologies  the two major competitors in this area  really are alumina and bgi  they only sequence fragments that are  relatively short and  taking these short fragments and looking  at genomes that is 45  repetitive is very very difficult all  the first generation mps certainly  aluminum bgi are very good for base  calling accuracy  but they're very poor for characterizing  large structural alterations  which are an important part of cancer  and many other disease states  there's other problems with the first  generation mps platforms  all are short we technology so again  it's difficult to generate the complete  genome sequence of a complex genome or  of a highly rearranged cancer genome  in addition you cannot when you use this  for rna sequencing  and looking at transcripts transcripts  many of them are considerably larger  than the sequence list  of the first generation technologies so  when you sequence  if you get a tiny little piece you don't  know which transcript isoform it came  from  so if you could sequence longer pieces  you could determine exactly which  isoform you're looking at  the other thing is that it's not  possible to directly use first  generation technologies to look at  genome-wide methylation with these  technologies  you have to take the genome sequence it  take that genome  bisulfite modify it to change all the  five methyl cytosines to uracil  and resequence it again so you actually  have to sequence the genome twice well  this leads rise to second generation mps  technologies  whereas all first generation  technologies which are highly dependent  on amplifying dna molecules  second generation technologies are  single molecule sequencing technologies  because you're doing single molecules  you don't have to amplify them  which means that you can look at much  larger pieces and because you're not  sequencing a copies of copies you can  actually sequence  much greater lengths and actually both  of the technology  that i'm not going to describe but i'm  just going to talk about briefly  can now sequence fragments that are  sometimes in excess of a million base  pairs in length  the two main platforms in the second  generation mps technologies  are pacific biosciences and we're very  pleased that dr jones carlos my friend  who's the chief scientific officer of  pac bio  is going to have a talk in in this  webinar where he's going to be talking  about some of the exciting things that  pat baier was doing in this sequencing  space  the second sequencing company is oxford  nanopore  and my colleague dr mark edward who's at  the mayo clinic  has been using oxford nanopore  sequencing to reveal portions of the  genome that are completely hidden  to short read technologies the last two  parts of the webinar that are part of  what i'm discussing are the clinical  uses of massively parallel sequencing  and my colleague dr matt ferber who's at  the mayo clinic  will talk about his work to develop  analytical and visual tools for  interpreting massively parallel sequence  data  my other colleague kevin holling also at  the mayo clinic  will talk about his work because he's  developed rna-seq rna sequencing  as a clinical assay to detect  translocations in cancer  i thank you for your time and your  attention  thank you dr smith for that outstanding  presentation  for additional informative presentations  view our agenda on our events page  as a final reminder our speaker will  follow up with any questions you've  submitted via email  thank you again for your participation
 

